          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "An HbA1c of 58 is written in mmol/mol, a unit many labs use. The guidance I searched gives the diagnostic threshold for diabetes as an HbA1c of **6.5 percent or above** (National diabetes association standards of care, educational summary). It says a result at that level is usually confirmed by a second test. The guidance I found does not give the mmol/mol equivalent of 6.5 percent, does not give the prediabetes range, and does not explain how to convert 58 mmol/mol into percentage units. A common conversion puts 58 mmol/mol at about 7.5 percent, but that conversion is not in the sources I could search, so please check it with your lab.\n\nBecause your result is above the 6.5 percent threshold on that conversion, please contact your clinician soon to discuss what it means and whether it needs a repeat test. Only a clinician can interpret your result.\n\nTo help me explain it better, could you tell me:\n1. Do you know which unit your lab reported? It may say \"mmol/mol\" or \"%\" next to the number.\n2. Do you have a diabetes diagnosis already, or is this result from a routine test?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
